3.2.3. X1.0 flare

The evolution of the X1.0 flare (F3) is shown by a few representative AIA 304 Å images in Figures 6(b)–(g). Prior

to the impulsive phase of the event, starting at ≈17:44 UT, we observe clear signature of preflare activity persisting

for ≈8 min (≈17:36–17:44 UT). This preflare phase is discernible in all the X-ray and EUV light curves (Figure

6(a)). This preflare activity is observed in the AIA images as enhanced brightening from the western part of the core

region, which also emits X-ray sources upto 25 keV (Figures 6(b)–(c)). Thereafter, the X-ray sources evolved at two

separate locations and we observe emission upto 25 keV from both the eastern and western parts of the flaring core

region (Figure 6(d)). The X-ray emission in the 6–12 keV energy range persisted in the eastern part of the core,

whereas, the strong emission in the energy range of 25–50 keV is observed to appear in the western part of the flaring

core (Figure 6(e)). During the peak of HXR light curves, we observe clear X-ray sources in the 50–100 keV energy

band. Importantly, this high energy source presents elongated structure with two distinct centroids (Figure 6(f)).

Subsequently, the X-ray emission upto 50 keV persisted, observed as a single source structure in multiple energy bands

(Figure 6(g)).

The evolution of F3 in the EUV 193 Å images is presented in Figure 7. Similar to the EUV 304 Å observations,

in these images also, we observe clear signature of enhanced brightening from the western part of the core revealing

the preflare activity (indicated by arrows in Figures 7(a)–(b)). Subsequently, this preflare enhancement spreads over

the whole core region (Figures 7(c)–(d)). During the main phase of the flare, intense and widespread flare emissions

are observed from the core (Figures 7(e)–(f)). Thereafter, the post-flare loop arcades are observed to form (shown by



